    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. the middleware idea is interesting, but i've checked all our services and they seem rock solid. \n\nit's just so hard to pin down because the error is so... small. like, it doesn't feel like a massive system crash, just this tiny, annoying little thing that grabs a request and pulls it out of the flow for a second. it feels almost like there's a little glitchy presence in the backend that's just playing tricks on us. \n\ndo you think there are any specific types of \"ghost\" errors in high-traffic APIs where something tiny and nearly invisible can cause these sudden, sudden hiccups without leaving a big footprint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Ghost\" errors in high-traffic APIs are often related to transient resource exhaustion or race conditions, which are incredibly hard to track down. Think of it like a tiny, invisible surge in the shared resources—like a sudden, brief spike in your server's available connections or memory pool. A race condition occurs when two processes try to update the same piece of data at the exact same millisecond, and one gets momentarily blocked or fails its transaction, causing that tiny hiccup. It's less of a bug and more of a timing conflict.",
      "source": "target",
